== Item 4: geometry
Blocks are the faces of one parity; array outline is |x|+|y| = n in lattice units (through the outer vertices), side n blocks.
Arctic circle x^2+y^2 = n^2/2 has radius n/2 blocks and touches the middles of the array's sides: it IS the inscribed circle.
K/n -> (1 - 1/sqrt2)/2 = 0.146447 ; frozen fraction -> 1 - pi/4 = 0.214602
Sign: finite-n frozen region is LARGER than the limit (K/n > 0.1464, frozen fraction > 0.2146) in my samples.
From memory of Johansson's theorem (radial fluctuation 2^(-5/6) n^(1/3) TW2 in lattice units; one diagonal block = 2 lattice units):
  K ~ 0.146447 n + 2^(-11/6) n^(1/3) (-TW2) + O(1):  mean excess -> 1.7711*0.2806 = 0.497 n^(1/3), sd -> 0.9018*0.2806 = 0.253 n^(1/3),
  mean excess / sd -> 1.964.  My 400 growths: excess = 0.406, 0.428, 0.445 n^(1/3) at 100, 200, 400 (se 0.013 each), i.e.
  0.497 n^(1/3) minus 0.42, 0.40, 0.38 blocks; sd = 0.261, 0.260, 0.265 n^(1/3); ratio 1.56, 1.65, 1.68 (rising).
